Interval.fromISOString parses ISO 8601 interval strings

std.datetime.interval.Interval.fromISOString now parses ISO 8601 interval
strings in both basic and extended formats. It supports intervals specified
as start/end time points or start/duration (and duration/end).

As with the date and time types in std.datetime, each value in a duration
must be within the range which its unit can hold (e.g. hours are at most
23), so a larger span must be expressed with the next larger designator,
e.g. $(D P1DT12H) rather than $(D PT36H).

Example:
-------
import std.datetime.date;
import std.datetime.interval;

auto i = Interval!Date.fromISOString("20030115/P1M");
assert(i == Interval!Date(Date(2003, 1, 15), Date(2003, 2, 15)));

auto j = Interval!DateTime.fromISOString("2003-01-15T12:00:00/PT1M");
assert(j == Interval!DateTime(DateTime(2003, 1, 15, 12, 0),
                              DateTime(2003, 1, 15, 12, 1)));

assert(Interval!Date.fromISOString("P1M/20030215") ==
       Interval!Date(Date(2003, 1, 15), Date(2003, 2, 15)));
-------